---
/**
 * Move a lever, see who it reaches — across all 609 districts.
 *
 * The one view that answers the question a policy argument actually turns on. A district page can
 * say what a change does to that district; only this can say how many districts move the other
 * way, and how that falls across property wealth.
 *
 * Unlike every other page here the figures are computed in the browser, because a lever has a
 * continuum of positions and a static page per position is not a page. What licenses that is the
 * verification gate in `src/scripts/scenario.ts`.
 */
import ScenarioControls from "../components/ScenarioControls.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadFeed } from "../lib/feed.ts";
import { count, millions } from "../lib/format.ts";
import * as routes from "../lib/routes.ts";
import { yearChip } from "../lib/year.ts";

const { bundle, forecastable } = loadFeed();
const s = bundle.statewide;
const meta = bundle.projection;
---

<Base
  title="Scenario"
  description={`Re-run Ohio's FY${bundle.fiscal_year} school funding formula with the guarantee, base cost, minimum state share and phase-in moved, across all ${s.districts} districts.`}
  image={routes.og.page("scenario")}
  imageAlt="Re-run Ohio's funding formula with the guarantee, base cost, minimum state share and phase-in moved"
  section="scenario"
>
  <h1>Scenario</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    Move a lever and re-run the department's FY{bundle.fiscal_year} model across all {
      count(s.districts)
    } districts. Current law pays {millions(s.realized_aid_total).replace("+", "")}, of which {
      millions(s.guarantee_total).replace("+", "")
    } is the guarantee.
  </p>

  {/*
    What every lever here holds fixed. Placed above the controls rather than below the results,
    because it is a limit on what the reader is about to do and not a footnote on what they got.
  */}
  <div class="card" data-part="held-fixed">
    <h2>What these levers hold fixed, and one they deliberately do not<Fragment set:html={yearChip("formula")} /></h2>
    <p class="note">
      Ohio's formula contains statewide constants — the average base cost per pupil, the statewide
      poverty share, the median weighted wealth — that are computed once over all districts. The
      department's own calculator warns that they are
      <strong>not recalculated</strong> when a district's data changes. That is right for a tool
      where you change one district and everything else stays put. It is wrong here, where every
      lever moves all {count(s.districts)} at once and the statewide average is the first thing
      that would move.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      So <strong>this model diverges from the department's on purpose</strong>. The
      <strong>cost input refresh</strong> lever scales base cost <em>and</em> the
      {millions(812_500_000).replace("+", "")} of categorical funding priced in it — special
      education, English learners and career-technical are each a weight times the average base
      cost per pupil, so they rise mechanically when it does. A 3.1% refresh moves
      {millions(113_000_000).replace("+", "")} of base cost aid and about
      {millions(25_200_000).replace("+", "")} more through those three. Until recently this page
      showed only the first number and said the second was missing.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Three things are still held still, for three different reasons. The two biggest categoricals
      — <a href={routes.wikiNode("formula-component", "fsfp-disadvantaged-pupil-impact-aid")}>DPIA</a>
      and
      <a href={routes.wikiNode("formula-component", "fsfp-targeted-assistance")}>targeted assistance</a>,
      {millions(1_890_000_000).replace("+", "")} together — divide a district's figure by a
      statewide one, so under a change that moves everything the numerator and denominator move
      together and genuinely cancel.
      <a href={routes.wikiNode("formula-component", "fsfp-gifted-units")}>Gifted</a> is priced in
      stated salaries rather than in base cost; a refresh would move those too, but by their own
      amount, and scaling them by this factor would be a guess wearing the shape of an identity.
      And the weighted half of
      <a href={routes.wikiNode("formula-component", "fsfp-preschool-special-education")}>preschool
      special education</a>, {millions(45_700_000).replace("+", "")}, is denominated the same way
      but sits outside foundation funding — outside everything on this page, not held fixed within
      it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div id="scenario-root">
    <ScenarioControls
      model={s.minimum_state_share}
      onGuarantee={s.on_guarantee}
      projection={{
        available: forecastable && meta != null,
        baseYear: meta?.base_year ?? 0,
        maxYear: meta?.horizon ?? 0,
      }}
    />

    <noscript>
      <div class="card err">
        <h2>This is the one page that needs JavaScript</h2>
        <p class="note">
          Every other page on this site carries its figures in the document and reads correctly
          without any script. This one re-derives Ohio's funding formula over all {
            count(s.districts)
          } districts as you move a slider, which is not something a pre-rendered page can do.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          The underlying data is still available: <a href="/data">the feed and a CSV of the panel</a>,
          and <a href="/districts">every district's published figures</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </noscript>

    <!--
      Three containers, and the order is the point.

      #scenario-out takes the simulation's headline tiles, directly under the levers, because that
      is where a reader who has just moved one looks. #projection-out takes the forecast. And
      #scenario-detail takes the rest of the simulation — the distribution, the most-affected
      table, the counts underneath — which are read at rest rather than at the moment a slider
      moves.

      The simulation is exact and the projection is a forecast. They are gated on separate checks
      and one can be disabled without the other, and there is deliberately no figure anywhere that
      adds them together. What that rule requires is that the forecast never appear above the
      figures it is a forecast *of*, and the tiles are still above it.
    -->
    <div id="scenario-out"></div>
    <div id="projection-out"></div>
    <div id="scenario-detail"></div>
    <p class="note" id="scenario-status"></p>
  </div>

  <script>
    import "../scripts/scenario.ts";
  </script>
</Base>
